      "id": 1,
      "text": "Selfridge was named after Thomas E. Selfridge, the first military aviator to die in an airplane accident.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.usacitiesonline.com/ndcountyselfridge.htm",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Selfridge was founded in 1911 along a Milwaukee Road branch line that split from the Pacific Extension.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selfridge%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1917, a stationary boxcar was moved into Selfridge on a flatcar to serve as the town's first depot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selfridge%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Milwaukee Road line that ran through Selfridge was abandoned in 1983, cutting off rail access.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selfridge%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Selfridge's first general store opened in 1911, followed by the Sioux Lumber Company the same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selfridge%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Early agriculture in Selfridge focused on flax cultivation and open-range cattle ranching.",
